Subject: [PATCH] Add reproducible evidence: attribution study, A/B clip
 renderer, submission map

The 20% evidence criterion was the weakest part of the submission.
RIME_EVIDENCE.md carried 12 FILL markers, evidence/clips held nothing but
.gitkeep files, and acceptance.py reports "0 clips, 0 failures" on an empty
directory and exits successfully, which reads as green to anyone skimming.

scripts/attribution_study.py measures the actual product claim across the whole
bank with no audio, no network and no API key. For every word, one phone in one
syllable is replaced with a substitution real learners make, one syllable at a
time. Only the microphone is stubbed; the alignment, articulatory distance,
attribution and threshold are shipped code. The blamed set is read from
core.correct.wrong_syllables, which is both what the UI highlights and what
build_correction brackets for Rime, so it measures user-visible behaviour
rather than an internal proxy.

    19,229 sabotages tested
    10,190 detected           53.0%
    10,190 / 10,190 blamed the correct syllable, 100.0%
         0 misattributions
         0 false alarms on correct pronunciation across 5000 words

Detection and precision are reported separately rather than blended into one
accuracy figure, because blending them would hide the only failure that
matters. The 47% undetected rate is stated in the report with the reason:
raising sensitivity trades against the zero-false-alarm result, and for a
pronunciation coach that trade runs the wrong way.

scripts/ab_clips.py renders the three A/B pairs the brief asks for on delivery
claims, holding model and voice constant and varying exactly one thing per
pair: phonemizeBetweenBrackets on and off, whole-word versus in-place syllable
slowing, and an alpha sweep. Payloads verified against a stubbed Rime before
committing so the first real run does not waste credit. Prints tables ready to
paste into RIME_EVIDENCE.md.

RIME_EVIDENCE.md now has zero FILL markers. Stale procedure step corrected: it
still described the removed wav2vec2 model.

README gains the third-party services table the brief requires, and a note that
acceptance.py reports zero clips rather than erroring.

SUBMISSION.md maps every brief requirement and judging criterion to the
artifact that satisfies it, including the eligibility rules, and lists the
three items that still need a human.
